WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.702
I open the Extensions view and find

00:02.702 --> 00:05.104
Deslop. This is the extension that adds

00:05.104 --> 00:07.506
the duplicate-code panel to VS Code. With

00:07.506 --> 00:09.908
it installed, I open this small project

00:09.908 --> 00:12.311
so we can review one finding from

00:12.311 --> 00:13.340
beginning to end.

00:14.140 --> 00:16.433
The Deslop panel lists the repeated code

00:16.433 --> 00:18.727
it found in this project. I click

00:18.727 --> 00:21.020
one finding to open its details. The

00:21.020 --> 00:23.313
file links tell me where the copies

00:23.313 --> 00:25.607
live, so I can go straight to

00:25.607 --> 00:27.900
the code instead of searching through folders.

00:28.700 --> 00:31.104
I open the first file, then the

00:31.104 --> 00:33.508
second one. Deslop takes me to the

00:33.508 --> 00:35.912
relevant lines in each file. I read

00:35.912 --> 00:38.316
the code around the highlighted section too,

00:38.316 --> 00:40.720
because two similar pieces of code may

00:40.720 --> 00:42.780
still be used in different ways.

00:43.580 --> 00:45.820
I use Compare to put the two

00:45.820 --> 00:48.060
copies side by side. Now I can

00:48.060 --> 00:50.300
see what stayed the same and what

00:50.300 --> 00:52.540
changed. This gives me a useful starting

00:52.540 --> 00:54.780
point for a cleanup, while I still

00:54.780 --> 00:56.700
decide which behavior the project needs.
